WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.680
- People of Gotham, 'tis I, Mark.

00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:04.840
Here to relieve you, the little people,

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:08.300
of the burden of wondering
who is the best Batman?

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:10.240
Before I continue, let's make sure that

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:11.940
we're all on the same page.

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:14.260
- There ain't no bat.

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:16.460
- [Mark] You are correct local ruffian.

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:18.220
There isn't one bat, there's been many

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:19.610
bat people over the years.

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:22.573
Batman, Batgirl, even Batboy.

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:25.860
- [Mr. Freeze] Stay cool, bird boy.

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:27.010
- [Mark] But with the recent news that

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:29.680
Robert Pattison will be
filling out the cape and cowl,

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:32.000
we decided it was high time to declare

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.890
a greatest Batman of them all.

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:36.420
Your contestants are

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:37.500
Adam West,

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:38.580
Michael Keaton,

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:39.570
Val Kilmer,

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:40.630
George Clooney,

00:00:40.630 --> 00:00:41.740
Kevin Conroy,

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:42.630
Christian Bale,

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:43.500
Ben Affleck,

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:44.562
and Will Arnett.

00:00:44.562 --> 00:00:45.450
(laughing)

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:47.680
Here's how we'll determine a winner.

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:49.920
Round one, box office.

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.210
Round two, Tomatometer.

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:54.680
Round three, most iconic.

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:56.690
And then, we'll do a wild card round.

00:00:56.690 --> 00:00:58.100
The crowd is at a fever pitch,

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:00.210
the villains are watching
from Arkham Asylum,

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:04.740
and the Batmen, they're at
home washing their tights.

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:05.573
Let's get it on.

00:01:05.573 --> 00:01:07.203
- To the Bat poles.

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:14.253
- Who's your tailor?

00:01:14.253 --> 00:01:15.867
- Does it come in black?

00:01:15.867 --> 00:01:16.700
(bell clangs)

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:18.660
- [Mark] Round one, box office.

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:20.490
This is going to be an easy win for

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:22.890
one of two possible contenders,

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:25.620
so before we name those
billionaire playboys,

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:27.990
let's take a gander at
some honorable mentions.

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:30.200
Ben Affleck step right up.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:31.820
You never got a chance to shine

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:33.810
in your very own Batman flick.

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:35.680
Apples which I do not like.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:37.497
However, he got to play Bats in both

00:01:37.497 --> 00:01:39.810
"Batman V. Superman: Dawn of Justice"

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:41.240
and in "Justice League".

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:45.900
Both flicks combined to gross
$593 million domestically

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.760
and $1.6 billion worldwide.

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:51.423
Not a bad effort at all,
especially when you consider

00:01:51.423 --> 00:01:54.590
that Val Kilmer and George
Clooney's combined outings

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:57.240
grossed $615 million here in the states

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:01.556
and $1.197 billion across the globe.

00:02:01.556 --> 00:02:04.660
(crowd cheering)

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:06.100
- Never leave the cave without it.

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:08.210
- [Mark] Unfortunately,
this is a biased category

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:11.620
for TV-friendly Batmen
Adam West and Kevin Conroy.

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:13.630
Their bread was mainly
buttered respectively

00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:17.110
on "Batman" the TV series and
"Batman: The Animated Series".

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:18.990
Adam West did get a feature film

00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:20.510
but it was just a different time

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:22.250
and I don't have the box office numbers

00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:24.090
for the 1966 flick.

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:25.730
Was Lincoln on the penny back then?

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:27.460
- Holy sardines!

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:29.300
- [Mark] And while Kevin
Conroy's Batman has

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:31.820
had two feature films,
they still do the bulk

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:36.350
of their damage on VHS, DVD,
Blu-ray, and streaming sales.

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:38.727
In theaters, his Batman
flicks have done a combined

00:02:38.727 --> 00:02:41.170
$16 million internationally.

00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:43.770
That's a lot of money
to me but not to Batman.

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:46.220
- What are your superpowers again?

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:47.100
- I'm rich.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:49.480
- [Mark] In cheerier
animated box office news,

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:52.690
Will Arnett's "Lego
Batman" did $179 million

00:02:52.690 --> 00:02:57.290
in the U.S. of A and $312
million over all of Earth.

00:02:57.290 --> 00:02:59.130
If you add in his appearances from the two

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:01.610
The Lego Movies, it
brings his worldwide total

00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:04.263
haul to an even one billion dollars.

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:07.447
And then, there were two.

00:03:07.447 --> 00:03:08.363
- I'm Batman.

00:03:08.363 --> 00:03:09.473
- I'm Batman.

00:03:09.473 --> 00:03:11.920
- [Mark] This is Keaton
versus Bale all the way.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:13.300
Bale did a full trilogy,

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:15.420
and Keaton, although he
was reportedly offered

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:18.270
a full dump truck of
100-dollar bills to come back

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:21.220
for a third time, only did "Batman 1989"

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:24.740
and its sequel, the accurately
titled, "Batman Returns".

00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:27.180
Bale's "The Dark Knight"
is the highest grossing

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:29.270
flick in the caped crusader's history

00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:32.480
getting $698 million domestically

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:35.110
and $1.3 billion worldwide.

00:03:35.110 --> 00:03:39.550
In second place, is "Batman
1989" with $590 million

00:03:39.550 --> 00:03:43.430
here in the states and $966
million internationally.

00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:48.430
All told, Bale's three
movies did $3.945 billion

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:52.350
in total Earth bucks
while Keaton's two talkies

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:54.753
add up to $1.56 billion.

00:03:54.753 --> 00:03:56.790
(clapping)

00:03:56.790 --> 00:03:59.133
So, why does Keaton win this round?

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:04.230
Well, quite simply if it
weren't for him and his Batman

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:06.600
we wouldn't have any of
these other Batfolk around.

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:07.610
Seriously.

00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:10.930
Both his films were massive
hits in way less theaters

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:12.260
than "The Dark Knight" trilogy

00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:15.420
and him saying no to a third Batman film

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:17.320
opened the door for our modern world

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:19.440
of quick superhero reinvention.

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:20.730
Val got his chance,

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:21.780
then George,

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:24.360
and George's movie was so
bad it was actually good.

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:27.030
- Why are all the gorgeous
ones homicidal maniacs?

00:04:27.030 --> 00:04:27.863
Is it me?

00:04:27.863 --> 00:04:29.430
- [Mark] And that we
got to do a total reboot

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:31.330
with a guy named Christopher
Nolan at the helm

00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:33.220
which gave us Christian Bale.

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:35.721
I'm so good at explaining stuff.

00:04:35.721 --> 00:04:37.560
I should've been a teacher.

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:39.580
Keaton's competitive per movie average

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:41.610
combined with the fact that
it was in less theaters

00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:42.710
with less showtimes,

00:04:42.710 --> 00:04:44.880
plus the fact that he got there first,

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:47.964
it all adds up to give
him the round one win.

00:04:47.964 --> 00:04:48.797
(bell clangs)

00:04:48.797 --> 00:04:49.630
Well done, Mikey.

00:04:50.470 --> 00:04:52.600
- Alfred, let's go shopping.

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:53.454
- Yes, sir.

00:04:53.454 --> 00:04:54.287
(bell clangs)

00:04:54.287 --> 00:04:56.122
- [Mark] Round two, Tomatometer.

00:04:56.122 --> 00:04:57.920
Unlike the criminals of Gotham City,

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:01.000
moviegoers tend to get excited
when Batman comes around.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:02.700
But what about the critics?

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:05.090
If Christopher Nolan's
trilogy is to be considered,

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:07.690
the Tomatometer seems to favor the Bat.

00:05:07.690 --> 00:05:09.150
Christian Bale's three flicks have

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:12.100
a super fresh average of 88%.

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:14.750
That's gotta be the top
of the mountain, right?

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:17.587
Actually, Will Arnett's
one standalone Bat flick,

00:05:17.587 --> 00:05:20.882
"The Lego Batman Movie",
is sitting at 90%.

00:05:20.882 --> 00:05:25.020
Plus, the original "Lego Movie" is at 96%.

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:26.400
- Relax everybody, I'm here.

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:28.780
- [Mark] But if we're
going by single Bat-centric

00:05:28.780 --> 00:05:31.164
movie accomplishments,
Christian Bale wins out again.

00:05:31.164 --> 00:05:35.360
His "The Dark Knight" is the king at 94%.

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:37.182
Ben Affleck's a cool Batman for sure,

00:05:37.182 --> 00:05:39.370
but his veggies have turned,

00:05:39.370 --> 00:05:42.300
as his two flicks average a rotten 34%.

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:43.440
He's still in good company,

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:46.400
as Val Kilmer's "Batman
Forever" is at 39%.

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:49.330
And maybe we can just
all agree to not even

00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:51.672
mention "Batman &amp; Robin"
during this segment.

00:05:51.672 --> 00:05:52.672
- Cowabunga!

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:57.030
- [Mark] Michael Keaton's
two flicks average 76%.

00:05:57.030 --> 00:05:59.730
And for funsies, guess
which one is higher?

00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:01.370
It's "Batman Returns".

00:06:01.370 --> 00:06:05.120
That's at 79% while the 1989
reintroduction of Batman

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:06.950
is at 74%.

00:06:06.950 --> 00:06:09.488
So, Bale's only real
competition here is with

00:06:09.488 --> 00:06:11.940
OG Adam West and Kevin Conroy.

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:15.030
Adam West's one Batman film is at 78%

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:16.550
but if you factor in the TV series,

00:06:16.550 --> 00:06:20.010
he leaps all the way up to 85.5%.

00:06:20.010 --> 00:06:22.170
Holy B average, Batman!

00:06:22.170 --> 00:06:23.350
- Holy hail storm!

00:06:23.350 --> 00:06:25.370
- [Mark] Kevin Conroy's
films have a fresh average

00:06:25.370 --> 00:06:28.990
of 62% but when you include
the animated shows he's in

00:06:28.990 --> 00:06:30.760
he jumps up to 80%.

00:06:30.760 --> 00:06:32.350
Good efforts all around.

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:34.930
And for my money, "Justice
League" isn't as bad

00:06:34.930 --> 00:06:36.700
as everyone says, you know what

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:38.050
I'm not dying on this hill again.

00:06:38.050 --> 00:06:40.900
- At some point even you
have to learn to move on.

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:42.490
- [Mark] When your worst outing as Batman

00:06:42.490 --> 00:06:45.300
is at 84% fresh on the Tomatometer

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:47.060
that means two things are true.

00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:48.757
Your name is Christian Bale and

00:06:48.757 --> 00:06:49.590
(bell clangs)

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:51.090
you just won round two.

00:06:51.090 --> 00:06:52.320
Well done.

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:53.840
- It's not who I am underneath

00:06:55.550 --> 00:06:58.733
but what I do that defines me.

00:06:59.953 --> 00:07:01.002
- Bruce?

00:07:01.002 --> 00:07:01.835
(bell clangs)

00:07:01.835 --> 00:07:03.660
- [Mark] Round three, most iconic.

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:07.070
Folks, as an icon myself,
I know being iconic

00:07:07.070 --> 00:07:09.630
is all about making memorable moments.

00:07:09.630 --> 00:07:11.780
Now, a memorable moment isn't always

00:07:11.780 --> 00:07:13.750
necessarily a good thing.

00:07:13.750 --> 00:07:17.210
Take Val Kilmer, solid Batman,
good brooding Bruce Wayne,

00:07:17.210 --> 00:07:20.470
but his most memorable
moment telling Alfred

00:07:20.470 --> 00:07:22.050
he'll get drive-through.

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:23.220
- I'll get drive-through.

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:24.410
- [Mark] C'mon, Bruce!

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:25.670
We're already watching your movie,

00:07:25.670 --> 00:07:27.100
we're going to Mickey D's after,

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:29.050
don't hit us over the head with it.

00:07:29.050 --> 00:07:30.610
- A man's gotta go his own way.

00:07:30.610 --> 00:07:32.480
- [Mark] For Michael Keaton,
his most iconic moment

00:07:32.480 --> 00:07:35.290
is a tie between a good
one and a crazy one.

00:07:35.290 --> 00:07:38.200
In the line that definitively
announced to the world

00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:40.480
that there is a new
superhero movie in town,

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:43.737
he grabs a Gotham street
tuff and simply says,

00:07:43.737 --> 00:07:44.710
"I'm Batman."

00:07:44.710 --> 00:07:46.250
That's how you scare a criminal.

00:07:46.250 --> 00:07:48.838
Also seen in "Batman
1989" is Michael Keaton

00:07:48.838 --> 00:07:51.620
reverting to his "Beetlejuice"
days when he says

00:07:51.620 --> 00:07:52.770
to the Joker--

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:54.231
- Now, you wanna get nuts?!

00:07:54.231 --> 00:07:55.893
C'mon, let's get nuts!

00:07:55.893 --> 00:07:58.290
- [Mark] What a great
moment for the whole family.

00:07:58.290 --> 00:08:00.360
Bring the kids, hell
we got plenty of snakes

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:02.060
and lizards for them to play with.

00:08:03.020 --> 00:08:03.853
- What?

00:08:03.853 --> 00:08:05.820
- [Mark] Some Batmen had
their best and worst moment

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:07.200
at the same time.

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:09.940
I got two words for you, shark spray.

00:08:09.940 --> 00:08:12.600
When a shark is gnawing on Batman's leg

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:15.160
and he's struggling to get
his handy shark repellent

00:08:15.160 --> 00:08:16.520
from his utility belt,

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:18.440
it's not only the stupidest thing ever,

00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:21.410
it's also a perfect
summation of the campiness

00:08:21.410 --> 00:08:23.730
that the original Batman
series was going for.

00:08:23.730 --> 00:08:25.395
Christopher Nolan's trilogy
was going for something

00:08:25.395 --> 00:08:28.710
darker as evidenced by one
of Bale's classic moments

00:08:28.710 --> 00:08:30.260
towards the end of "The Dark Knight".

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:31.093
Rachel!

00:08:31.093 --> 00:08:32.280
- Where are they?!

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:34.330
- [Mark] Bale's at his most
iconic when he's straining

00:08:34.330 --> 00:08:36.370
his voice so hard as the Caped Crusader

00:08:36.370 --> 00:08:38.360
that it borders on insanity.

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:40.100
I'm not wearing hockey pants!

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:42.090
Okay, the Joker interrogation scene

00:08:42.090 --> 00:08:45.740
and his escape from Ra's al
Ghul's home for wayward boys

00:08:45.740 --> 00:08:47.112
is pretty sweet, too.

00:08:47.112 --> 00:08:50.781
- You either die a hero
or you live long enough

00:08:50.781 --> 00:08:52.857
to see yourself become the villain.

00:08:52.857 --> 00:08:56.600
- [Mark] Speaking of dark, how
about Kevin Conroy's Batman?

00:08:56.600 --> 00:08:59.570
Broody-to-the-max and
he's got some killer lines

00:08:59.570 --> 00:09:01.913
like in "The Mask of the Phantasm".

00:09:01.913 --> 00:09:03.550
- I didn't count on being happy.

00:09:03.550 --> 00:09:06.084
- [Mark] Or my personal favorite
from "The Animated Series".

00:09:06.084 --> 00:09:08.187
- I am vengeance.

00:09:08.187 --> 00:09:09.964
I am the night.

00:09:09.964 --> 00:09:10.964
I am Batman!

00:09:13.238 --> 00:09:14.080
- [Mark] Woo!

00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:17.100
You open with that, it's
an instant swipe right.

00:09:17.100 --> 00:09:19.610
Ladies, am I wrong here?

00:09:19.610 --> 00:09:22.160
Rounding out the Batfolk,
George Clooney really

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:23.680
only has dull moments.

00:09:23.680 --> 00:09:25.810
Will Arnett has some genuinely funny stuff

00:09:25.810 --> 00:09:28.050
and is a great study of the Batman pathos,

00:09:28.050 --> 00:09:30.800
but the only real contender
here is Ben Affleck.

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:33.950
Ben has a great moment early
on in "Batman v Superman"

00:09:33.950 --> 00:09:35.600
when he's running towards danger

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:37.250
as everyone else is fleeing.

00:09:37.250 --> 00:09:40.670
Dude really fills out a
Men's Wearhouse vest nicely.

00:09:40.670 --> 00:09:42.790
However, by the end of
that flick we're all ready

00:09:42.790 --> 00:09:44.910
for the big climax and then what happens?

00:09:44.910 --> 00:09:46.460
Martha!

00:09:46.460 --> 00:09:48.010
That's what stops you, dude?

00:09:48.010 --> 00:09:50.950
You're killing an alien,
you got him on the ropes,

00:09:50.950 --> 00:09:52.017
your ma could show up,

00:09:52.017 --> 00:09:54.030
"Brucey, stop hurting the poor thing,"

00:09:54.030 --> 00:09:55.730
and you'd still finish him off.

00:09:55.730 --> 00:09:56.883
C'mon man!

00:09:59.720 --> 00:10:01.600
- Don't believe everything you hear, son.

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:03.070
- [Mark] It's a brawl
between Christian Bale

00:10:03.070 --> 00:10:04.350
and Michael Keaton for this round

00:10:04.350 --> 00:10:07.520
and you know what, it's
actually Kevin Conroy.

00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:09.070
That's your winner right here.

00:10:09.070 --> 00:10:11.360
Maybe he wasn't on as many magazine covers

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:13.140
when his animated movies came out,

00:10:13.140 --> 00:10:15.470
but if we're talking about iconic moments

00:10:15.470 --> 00:10:17.580
there's one in practically every episode

00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:19.120
of "Batman: the Animated Series",

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:21.710
and every movie he's in is jam-packed with

00:10:21.710 --> 00:10:23.540
Bruce Bat's conflict.

00:10:23.540 --> 00:10:25.260
With his films and the show,

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:26.800
he had the most opportunity,

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:29.000
the most amount of canvas
with which to paint,

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:32.118
and he gets to go against the
best Joker ever, Mark Hamill.

00:10:32.118 --> 00:10:32.951
(bell clangs)

00:10:32.951 --> 00:10:34.043
Conroy wins round three.

00:10:37.968 --> 00:10:40.299
♪ Am I blue ♪

00:10:40.299 --> 00:10:41.540
(bell clangs)

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:43.870
- [Mark] And now it's time
for the wild card round,

00:10:43.870 --> 00:10:45.970
the greatest social impact.

00:10:45.970 --> 00:10:47.690
Now, this one is gonna Michael Keaton

00:10:47.690 --> 00:10:48.930
versus Christian Bale.

00:10:48.930 --> 00:10:50.300
Kevin Conroy is great,

00:10:50.300 --> 00:10:51.510
West is a legend,

00:10:51.510 --> 00:10:53.060
the others had a nice time.

00:10:53.060 --> 00:10:54.360
- Oh shit.

00:10:54.360 --> 00:10:56.120
- [Mark] But if we're
looking at who influenced

00:10:56.120 --> 00:10:58.860
the landscape with their
greatest detective more

00:10:58.860 --> 00:11:01.160
it comes down to these two hunks.

00:11:01.160 --> 00:11:03.230
Michael Keaton gets some
points for what went down

00:11:03.230 --> 00:11:05.330
even before he started shooting.

00:11:05.330 --> 00:11:08.070
Once it was announced that
Mr. Mom was gonna be Batman,

00:11:08.070 --> 00:11:10.020
people lost their minds.

00:11:10.020 --> 00:11:11.750
It was the first time in the modern era

00:11:11.750 --> 00:11:13.850
that a true shit was flipped over

00:11:13.850 --> 00:11:15.380
a comic book movie casting.

00:11:15.380 --> 00:11:19.100
50,000 fans sent in protest letters

00:11:19.100 --> 00:11:23.590
hoping to convince Tim Burton
to fire Johnny Dangerously.

00:11:23.590 --> 00:11:25.350
And this was before the internet.

00:11:25.350 --> 00:11:27.100
You actually had to like write a letter,

00:11:27.100 --> 00:11:29.573
and put a stamp on it,
and send it somewhere.

00:11:29.573 --> 00:11:32.400
The fans really hated this idea initially,

00:11:32.400 --> 00:11:35.120
but then, from opening night on people saw

00:11:35.120 --> 00:11:38.070
what a brilliant actor can
do with this role of duality

00:11:38.070 --> 00:11:39.740
and Keaton cemented himself as a

00:11:39.740 --> 00:11:41.910
versatile A-list movie star.

00:11:41.910 --> 00:11:43.557
- I want you to tell all
your friends about me.

00:11:43.557 --> 00:11:47.160
- [Mark] "Batman 1989" as a
film was a cultural landmark.

00:11:47.160 --> 00:11:49.550
All summer long, it was Batmania.

00:11:49.550 --> 00:11:51.120
I know because I lived it.

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:53.280
Did you know there was a
run on black cotton T-shirts

00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:55.740
because so many people were buying them up

00:11:55.740 --> 00:11:57.920
to make contraband Batman shirts.

00:11:57.920 --> 00:11:59.880
I probably owned one or nine.

00:11:59.880 --> 00:12:01.610
It ruled the entire year.

00:12:01.610 --> 00:12:03.800
And I also remember in the summer of 1992,

00:12:03.800 --> 00:12:06.670
a bunch of parents freaking
out because The Penguin

00:12:06.670 --> 00:12:10.740
bit a guy's nose and blood
goes gushing everywhere.

00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:13.010
So, apparently, parents
were cool with Batman

00:12:13.010 --> 00:12:15.620
murdering a gaggle of
killer clowns 20 minutes ago

00:12:15.620 --> 00:12:18.280
but a little plasma was over the line.

00:12:18.280 --> 00:12:19.870
Ah parenting.

00:12:19.870 --> 00:12:21.270
Hope it never happens to me.

00:12:23.030 --> 00:12:25.310
Christian Bale steps into
a very different world

00:12:25.310 --> 00:12:26.170
than Michael Keaton.

00:12:26.170 --> 00:12:28.920
In 2005, audiences were primed and ready

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:31.090
for a darker, grittier type of hero.

00:12:31.090 --> 00:12:34.380
And who better to give that
to them than American Psycho.

00:12:34.380 --> 00:12:36.050
Patrick Bateman led a double life,

00:12:36.050 --> 00:12:37.357
so does Bruce Wayne.

00:12:37.357 --> 00:12:40.690
"Batman Begins", aside from
being my personal favorite

00:12:40.690 --> 00:12:43.690
comic book film ever, also
helped usher in a new dawn

00:12:43.690 --> 00:12:44.990
for the superhero movie.

00:12:44.990 --> 00:12:47.970
It was part of a tidal wave
that we're still surfing today.

00:12:47.970 --> 00:12:49.850
But it wasn't until "The Dark Knight" that

00:12:49.850 --> 00:12:51.612
the social impact was truly felt.

00:12:51.612 --> 00:12:53.843
- Why so serious?

00:12:54.887 --> 00:12:56.450
- [Mark] "The Dark Knight"
showed movie critics

00:12:56.450 --> 00:12:58.580
what fans had hoped for all along.

00:12:58.580 --> 00:13:01.530
These kind of movies could
be taken just as seriously

00:13:01.530 --> 00:13:04.280
as cinema as any other flick.

00:13:04.280 --> 00:13:06.530
It taught us that the term superhero movie

00:13:06.530 --> 00:13:09.500
wasn't necessarily a
hindrance to award chances.

00:13:09.500 --> 00:13:12.780
In fact, because the source
material was so fantastical

00:13:12.780 --> 00:13:15.410
these types of roles
would allow serious actors

00:13:15.410 --> 00:13:18.440
to show off their chops
in new, inventive ways.

00:13:18.440 --> 00:13:20.250
So, how does one choose between these two,

00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:22.030
when both were part of nothing less than

00:13:22.030 --> 00:13:24.060
an entire paradigm shift?

00:13:24.060 --> 00:13:26.390
Well, because we're looking
at Batman specifically,

00:13:26.390 --> 00:13:28.437
and while Christian
Bale was a huge part of

00:13:28.437 --> 00:13:30.180
"The Dark Knight" trilogy's greatness,

00:13:30.180 --> 00:13:32.140
the lion's share of
praise for these films,

00:13:32.140 --> 00:13:34.810
standing head and shoulders
above previous Batman efforts

00:13:34.810 --> 00:13:36.590
was thanks to Christopher Nolan.

00:13:36.590 --> 00:13:38.960
And it was Heath Ledger's
Joker that led the way

00:13:38.960 --> 00:13:41.300
for films like this to
be critically-acclaimed.

00:13:41.300 --> 00:13:43.100
So, while Michael Keaton
certainly had help

00:13:43.100 --> 00:13:45.020
from the likes of Tim
Burton, Jack Nicholson,

00:13:45.020 --> 00:13:47.870
and Michelle Pfeiffer, it
was his genius portrayal

00:13:47.870 --> 00:13:50.480
of a tortured soul that
redefined what an audience

00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:52.300
would expect from a superhero.

00:13:52.300 --> 00:13:54.170
Michael Keaton is a great Bruce Wayne,

00:13:54.170 --> 00:13:55.640
an awesome crimefighter,

00:13:55.640 --> 00:13:57.848
in short, the best Batman in history.

00:13:57.848 --> 00:13:58.681
(bell clangs)

00:13:58.681 --> 00:14:00.483
He wins this round and he wins the match.

00:14:04.190 --> 00:14:07.400
Yep, that Robert Pattinson
has some big boots to fill.

00:14:07.400 --> 00:14:09.410
Be it from any of the previous Batfolk

00:14:09.410 --> 00:14:11.020
that had to figure out how to pee

00:14:11.020 --> 00:14:13.320
while wearing that cumbersome suit.

00:14:13.320 --> 00:14:16.550
Just for some fun, Mark
Hoffmeyer, the Alfred to my Robin,

00:14:16.550 --> 00:14:19.050
compiled a bunch of lists
ranking the best Batman

00:14:19.050 --> 00:14:20.810
from slew of different polls.

00:14:20.810 --> 00:14:23.210
His results show that it's
actually Christian Bale

00:14:23.210 --> 00:14:25.330
who takes the mantle as the Bat GOAT

00:14:25.330 --> 00:14:27.710
and that Kevin Conroy
is a silver medalist.

00:14:27.710 --> 00:14:28.880
Keaton was a close third,

00:14:28.880 --> 00:14:30.383
but it just proves, what?

00:14:30.383 --> 00:14:33.100
No, it does not prove that I'm a moron.

00:14:33.100 --> 00:14:36.100
It proves that our own
opinions matter the most.

00:14:36.100 --> 00:14:38.600
So, now it's your turn, dear viewer.

00:14:38.600 --> 00:14:40.450
Comment below with the
person who you think

00:14:40.450 --> 00:14:42.070
is the best Batman of all time.

00:14:42.070 --> 00:14:43.110
Is it Michael Keaton?

00:14:43.110 --> 00:14:44.310
Is it Kevin Conroy?

00:14:44.310 --> 00:14:46.170
Nicholas Cage as Big Daddy?

00:14:46.170 --> 00:14:48.260
Who you got and defend your answer.

00:14:48.260 --> 00:14:49.750
And while you're at it, do you think

00:14:49.750 --> 00:14:52.210
Robert Pattinson has
the chops to add another

00:14:52.210 --> 00:14:54.400
exciting chapter in the Batman legacy?

00:14:54.400 --> 00:14:58.367
Remember Pattinson you
are my number one guy.

00:15:00.820 --> 00:15:02.470
- [Thomas] All right, have it your way.

00:15:02.470 --> 00:15:03.500
- [Mark] That's all from me today.

00:15:03.500 --> 00:15:06.240
I'm taking off and I'm a
little hungry but it's okay

00:15:06.240 --> 00:15:07.299
I'll get drive-through.

00:15:07.299 --> 00:15:09.882
(upbeat music)

